WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.330
Oh, hi there I'm Gavin.

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:02.410
Welcome to "The Slow Mo Guys".

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:04.360
Today we'll be using slow motion

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.090
to learn how a film camera works.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:09.150
A film camera for movies, not for stills.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:11.040
This is a 16 millimeter film camera.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:11.970
It's Russian.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:16.200
It's called a Krasnogorsk-3,
which I definitely just read.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.100
And didn't have to Google
how to pronounce it.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.750
We'll be loading up with
some 16 millimeter film

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.600
and using the Phantom
to show all its magic.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.180
Imagery is usually captured
onto film through a lens

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.330
with a photo sensitive chemical reaction.

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:30.000
You can see that the light hitting

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.440
this film has darkened it.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:32.650
It's a color negative film.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:36.370
So areas of very bright light
will burn in a dark image

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.740
and that'll then be flipped
in the development process.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.790
And you can see here,
the area I left behind

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.390
it's slowly darkening
as light from the room,

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:45.870
exposes it and I can speed that up here.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.470
If you were then to develop that,

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:49.600
you'd end up with something
that looks like this.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.310
First thing, I'm gonna do is load

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:52.740
this bad boy with some films.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.330
I'm gonna turn the frame
rate down to very low,

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:56.460
eight frames a second.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.450
Put a little bit of
tension in the old spring.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.230
It's a windup camera.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:02.763
It's pressure plate comes out.

00:01:05.402 --> 00:01:07.030
This camera has single perf reels.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:08.850
So that's the type of film
we're gonna put in it.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.630
It basically just means
the sprocket holes down

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.680
only one side of the film,
and you can fit either sound

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.440
or use a format called super
16 and shoot a wider image

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.040
if your camera has a wider gate.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.550
So that's the type of
film you've got here.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.430
Just a single perf 16 millimeter film.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.130
Even though the image size is different

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:26.590
between these two formats,

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:30.420
the width of the film itself
is indeed 16 millimeters.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:32.690
Okay, this is now loaded,
the films on this one.

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:34.590
It's gonna be taken up by this one.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.760
So it'll end up here
and it's looped through,

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:39.430
going through here, this pressure plate

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:44.133
is keeping the film flat
against the focal plane.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.478
And when running it looks
something like this.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.620
You would never wanna usually load

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.420
a film camera in such a bright room.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.820
Next thing to do, is to pop this back on.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.660
A lot of people would also tape around

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.290
to prevent light leakage.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:00.310
Now that we're loaded up with film

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.750
we can set the frame
rates, so we'll do 24,

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.240
which is the standard
recording frame rate of film.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.030
This camera will actually
go all the way up to 48.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:09.863
Okay.

00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:11.340
Lenses off.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.600
I will now use a camera,
to film me using a camera,

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:15.683
to film a camera.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:18.730
Here's a selfie I took on a camera

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:20.722
of me using a camera to film me using...

00:02:20.722 --> 00:02:21.555
All right.

00:02:21.555 --> 00:02:22.388
I've just realized I can actually take

00:02:22.388 --> 00:02:23.560
more of this off to show more.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:24.490
There we go.

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.640
As we learned with our Slow Mo DSLR video

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.760
a 45 degree mirror, we'll let
you look through the lens.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.200
The mirror we'll then move out of the way

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.980
revealing the shutter doors,
which also move out of the way.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.440
And the image will be
exposed on the sensor.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.070
Then the shutter closes, then
the mirror comes back down.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:41.730
with this film camera it's
a very similar principle.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.020
When you look through the viewfinder

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.390
you get the correct perspective

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.500
because you're looking
through the actual lens.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.720
This is done by bouncing
the image off these

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.820
beam splitting mirrors
and off this mirror disc

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:54.980
placed at 45 degrees, very
similar to the stills camera.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.860
What you're seeing here,
isn't the film plane.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:00.360
It's actually a path back to
the viewfinder on the back.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.950
This system differs slightly
from the DSLR in that

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:06.670
this mirror doesn't move out
of the way to reveal a shutter

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:09.390
that then opens to reveal the film plane.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.060
This mirror is the shutter itself.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.753
This is a rotary disc shutter,
and this is how it works.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.290
This camera is rolling
at 24 frames a second.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.270
And the gap in the shutter disk

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.100
determines the shutter speed.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.410
For every half rotation of
this disk, which is one frame,

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:33.050
the film is exposed for
one 60th of a second

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.500
and that is determined
by the size of the gap

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:37.240
in the shutter there.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:38.770
This is what you would
see if you were looking

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:41.530
through the viewfinder, at me
pouring water on the ground.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.060
If you could also see in slow motion

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:44.099
this is what it would look like.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:49.050
You can see here side by side,
that the areas of darkness

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:52.130
in the viewfinder are actually
the exposures on the film.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:54.750
You cannot see through the viewfinder

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.710
when light is hitting the film itself.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:59.330
So you're basically seeing
the opposite cycles.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:00.970
This is what it looks like from the inside

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:03.810
as film is being dragged through the gate.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.090
You may remember that any
movement in your subject

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.700
while the shutter is open
will cause motion blur

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.380
but a DSLR sensor is stationary.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.660
If films constantly
moving, how do you avoid

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:16.150
every single frame being a complete blur

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:18.160
as the film is pulled through the camera?

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:19.840
To show you how this clever part works,

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.330
I'm gonna write numbers on each frame.

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:25.430
This is one second a film
at 24 frames a second.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:26.830
And we're gonna run
this through the camera

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:29.780
so we can see what's actually
happening with the film.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.550
All the slow-mo in this
video is being captured

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:33.570
a thousand frames, a second in 4K

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.340
which is approximately 40 times slower.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:39.163
Here we go, one second through the camera.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.570
This is some absolutely
amazing engineering here.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:49.510
Every single exposure
on every frame of film

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:52.380
the film is perfectly stationary.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.420
And by the time the shutter
comes around for a new exposure

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:59.453
the film has moved without us
even seeing to the next frame.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.280
In order to see how this works

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:06.560
I'm gonna take the disc off entirely.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.253
So you can now see the film at all times.

00:05:14.872 --> 00:05:18.480
Without the shutter, you
can see when the film moves.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.390
The whole mechanism is timed so perfectly

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:24.250
that as the shutter covers the film

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:26.990
the film is moved down,
which prevents any streaking

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:28.930
or motion blur from the film itself.

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:32.140
If I superimpose the shutter back on

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:35.163
you can see it only moves
when it's under the mirror.

00:05:35.163 --> 00:05:36.870
This is so fascinating to me.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.150
And because the shutter and
the film pull-down speed

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.660
are linked no matter
what frame rate you shoot

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:43.317
this will always be the case.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:46.450
So now I want to see the mechanism

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:49.120
that is pulling the film down every time.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:51.227
And in order to get a better view of that,

00:05:51.227 --> 00:05:53.480
I'm gonna take off this piece,

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:56.570
which should reveal the pull-down claw.

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:59.160
This lens makes everything look ginormous.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.330
So you can see, as I press record here,

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:03.560
this mechanism in the
middle is leaning forward

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:04.590
at the top of its cycle.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.970
And as it leans forward, this claw

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:10.650
goes perfectly into the
Sprocket holes with the film

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:14.343
pulling it down into the perfect
position for each exposure.

00:06:24.072 --> 00:06:25.990
I'm very impressed this
camera's still works honestly.

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:28.150
They started production in 1971.

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.330
So that's a lot of moving
parts that you're asking

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:33.120
to last half a century at this point.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.330
Just for the heck of it
now I'm going to write

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:36.560
frame numbers on again, but
this time for a second's

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:39.150
worth of film at 48 frames a second.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.100
So everything in the
camera will be working

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.433
at double the speed now.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.080
If you projected this
back at 24 frames a second

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.350
you would get half speed slow motion.

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:59.670
Or if you projected it
back at 48 frames a second

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:02.103
you get some very Hobbity looking footage.

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:07.080
And there we have it, a
wonderful slow-mo look

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:09.960
at some old school analog tech.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:10.900
I love it.

